# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Qabandi — the configuration every CI job starts from.
#
# Committed on purpose, and holding nothing that is secret anywhere: a
# throwaway internal token and the database settings that are the same wherever
# there is a database.
#
# ## Why this file exists
#
# The workflow used to configure each job from its own `env:` block, and the
# blocks drifted. `apps/service/src/config.ts` parses `process.env` at import
# and calls `process.exit(1)` when the shape is wrong, so a checkout with no
# `.env` — which is exactly what CI is — killed the whole service suite before
# a single test body ran. It had therefore never once executed in CI, and
# nothing said so: the web tests failed earlier in the same job, so the step
# was skipped rather than reached.
#
# One file, copied to `.env` by every job, removes that class. It also gives a
# developer a way to stand up a CI-shaped environment:
#
#     cp .env.ci .env      # then add the connection strings for your database
#
# Precedence is on our side: `loadEnv()` never overwrites a variable that is
# already set, so a job's own `env:` block still wins over everything here.
#
# ## What is deliberately NOT here, and why
#
# **`SERVICE_URL`.** No CI job runs `apps/service`. Pointing the web app at a
# port nothing listens on is worse than leaving it unset, because the code
# distinguishes the two: `service()` in `app/api/assistant/route.ts` returns
# null unless *both* `SERVICE_URL` and `INTERNAL_API_SECRET` are present, and
# the assistant answers "not configured" on that path — which is the behaviour
# `assistant.spec.ts` asserts. Setting it would turn that deterministic answer
# into a connection attempt against a dead port.
#
# **Connection strings.** They are job topology, not configuration: four of the
# six jobs have no database at all, and the two that do point at their own
# service container. They belong beside the `services:` block that creates them.
#
# **`BETTER_AUTH_SECRET`.** Generated per run and appended to the environment by
# the jobs that boot the app. A fixed base64 string in a committed file is a key
# that looks real, gets copied into a deployment, and signs sessions somebody
# else can mint.
#
# **`QABANDI_PILOT_FIXTURES`.** Either setting would be wrong for some job. `0`
# disables the fixtures unconditionally, and the unit job has no database — its
# route tests run *on* the fixtures. `1` would shadow the database in the two
# jobs that have one. Unset, `usePilotFixtures()` decides from `DATABASE_URL`,
# which is the right answer in all six.
#
# **`NODE_ENV`.** `next build` and `next start` set their own, and a value here
# would leak into the build.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Authenticates web -> service calls that carry no user session. Nothing in CI
# runs the service over a network; this only has to be present and non-empty,
# because `config.ts` requires it and the service suite cannot import without
# it. Harmless to the web app, which needs `SERVICE_URL` too before it will
# call anything, and does not have one.
INTERNAL_API_SECRET=ci-only-internal-api-secret

# --- Database --------------------------------------------------------------
# The connection strings come from the workflow; these are the settings that
# are the same wherever there is a database.
#
# `disable` is a decision rather than an omission. The service container is on
# the runner's own loopback interface and the packets never leave the machine,
# and `resolveSsl()` refuses to start in production against a remote host with
# no choice made — writing this one down is what keeps that refusal meaningful.
DATABASE_SSL=disable
DATABASE_POOLED=false
DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10
